---
import architecture from '../assets/wristband-exploded-clean.png';
import ComponentStudy from './ComponentStudy.astro';

const parts = [
  {
    name: 'Multispectral sensor',
    role: 'Captures optical wavelength bands. Whether the extra bands help recognize unreliable readings is the research question of this project.',
    tag: 'Experimental',
  },
  { name: 'PPG sensor', role: 'Captures the pulse wave, from which heart rate is estimated.', tag: null },
  {
    name: 'Skin-temperature sensor',
    role: 'Tracks the trend of skin temperature. It is not core body temperature.',
    tag: null,
  },
  { name: 'Accelerometer', role: 'Detects movement and motion artifacts, so affected readings can be set aside.', tag: null },
  { name: 'Microcontroller and radio', role: 'Collects the readings and transmits them locally to the onboard server.', tag: null },
  { name: 'Battery and power management', role: 'Powers sensing on the wrist.', tag: null },
];
---
<section class="section product-section" id="wristband">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / Wristband</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">Screenless sensing. Continuous contact.</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    A continuous band. A discreet sensor array. The proposed wristband gathers signals
    without adding another screen to the astronaut’s day.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no screen and nothing to operate. A titanium outer shell and a soft inner liner
    hold a single sensor module on the inner face of the band. The band measures; it does
    not interpret. Analysis happens on the server, a short wireless hop away.
  </p>

  <ComponentStudy image={architecture} parts={parts} title="Wristband sensor assembly"
    alt="Exploded view of the Spectra wristband: titanium shell, flexible electronics and skin-contact sensor liner." />

  <p>
    A multispectral sensor measures the spectral content of light. That alone does not make
    it a validated measure of glucose, hydration or disease, and Spectra does not claim it is.
  </p>


</section>
